// Regression (step 231 / code-review finding; semantics REVISED by the
// spawn-throw-and-reentrancy task, LEDGER item 16): a `throw` that unwinds
// out of a spawned thread's entry function must not vanish silently. The
// runtime reports "kryos: uncaught exception in spawned thread: <msg>" to
// stderr -- and, as of this revision, the WHOLE PROCESS then terminates
// (exit 101, the same contract an uncaught `throw` on the main thread
// already has), so this program now exits 101 and "main alive" is NOT
// printed. This is a deliberate change from the original "thread dies,
// process lives" isolation: Kryos exceptions are a thread-local flag
// checked after every call site with a synthesized early-return, not
// native stack unwinding, so "thread dies" meant every statement written
// AFTER the throw point in that task's own body was silently skipped --
// including, commonly, a paired `wg_done`/`chan_send`/actor-notify signal
// a `WaitGroup`/channel consumer elsewhere was blocked waiting for, which
// hung forever with no diagnostic connecting the two
// (`attack_spawn_uncaught_throw_waitgroup_hang.kry`). Treating an uncaught
// spawn-task `throw` as fatal to the whole process converts that silent
// permanent hang into an immediate, attributable, non-zero exit. See
// docs/09-concurrency.md's "Error handling in spawned blocks" section.
// Moved out of tests/smoke/ into tests/security/ (was
// tests/smoke/test_spawn_throw_reports.kry): the parity runner
// (tests/parity/run_parity.sh) treats ANY non-zero exit as FAIL_RUN and
// only tests/smoke/ programs are expected to exit 0, so a test whose
// CORRECT behavior is now a deliberate non-zero exit does not belong
// there anymore. Both backends must still agree it's exit 101 -- verified
// manually both ways as part of the spawn-throw-and-reentrancy fix rather
// than via the parity harness.

fn worker() {
    throw "thread exploded"
}

fn main() {
    spawn {
        worker()
    }
    sleep_ms(200)
    println("main alive")
}
